#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153451 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 7 & ≡9 \\cdot 2 & (mod5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5$.\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$,\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3e0278c-44da-546e-bc47-9529380f4b5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9017 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153463 :tutorial/content "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "5778b7fb-626e-59d3-ad81-8d574bb2b4e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9040 :tutorial/title "The Multiplicative Property"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153469 :tutorial/content "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$. By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain\n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5dad198-84e6-5eab-9d7e-6df354768fce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9076 :tutorial/title "The Exponentiation Property"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153473 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following properties:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's prove the first property. The proof of the second property is similar.\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the scalar multiplication property from the definition of congruence. First, note that we have the following chain of implications for $k, p\\in\\mathbb Z$:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟹a - b = pn; ⟹k(a - b) = kpn; ⟹ka - kb = (kp)n\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nkp = q\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nka - kb = (kp)n \\mid ⟹ka - kb = qn; ⟹ka≡kb(modn)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof of the second property is very similar.\n- We'll prove the multiplicative and exponentiation properties in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "5cf4e7b1-34e6-55ea-bb8f-ae13a8fa29d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9125 :tutorial/title "Proving the Scalar Multiplication Property"}]}
